# Guards

A guard is a check that runs before a handler. It either rejects the
request, and the handler never runs, or it succeeds and hands the handler a
typed value. The usual example is signing in: the guard works out who is
making the request, answers `401` if it cannot, and otherwise gives every
handler behind it a `User`.

That second half is what makes a guard more than a filter. The handler does
not look the user up again, or deal with the case where there is not one. It
cannot be reached without a user, and the compiler knows it.

## Writing a guard

A guard is a function from the context to a `service.Result`. Return
`Ok(value)` to let the request through, or an error from `howdy/service` to
reject it:

```gleam
import gleam/http/request
import gleam/list
import howdy/context.{type Context}
import howdy/service

pub type User {
  User(id: Int, name: String, admin: Bool)
}

pub fn authenticated(ctx: Context(a)) -> service.Result(User) {
  let cookies = request.get_cookies(ctx.request)
  case list.key_find(cookies, "session") {
    Ok(session_id) -> find_session_user(session_id)
    Error(Nil) -> Error(service.Unauthorized)
  }
}
```

Here `find_session_user` stands for your own lookup, returning `Ok(user)`
for a live session and `Error(service.Unauthorized)` otherwise. If you would
rather not write sign-in yourself, [Howdy Auth](/docs/auth/overview) comes
with this guard ready made, as `auth.required`.

A guard returns a `Result`, not a response, so it cannot use the `use`
helpers from `howdy/cookie` and `howdy/query`, which answer the request
themselves. Read the request directly, as above, with `request.get_cookies`,
`request.get_header` or `request.get_query`.

The error decides the response. `service.Unauthorized` answers `401` with
`{"error":"unauthorized","code":"unauthorized"}`, and `service.Forbidden` answers `403`. Any error
in [Services and errors](/docs/controllers/services) will do.

A check with nothing useful to hand on returns `Ok(Nil)`.

## Guarding a controller

`controller.guarded` takes a guard along with the prefix. The guard runs
before every route on the controller, and its value is on the context as
`ctx.guard`:

```gleam
import howdy/controller

pub fn account_controller() {
  controller.guarded("account", authenticated)
  |> controller.get("/", fn(ctx) {
    controller.text(ctx, "Hello, " <> ctx.guard.name)
  })
  |> controller.post("/email", change_email)
  |> controller.build
}
```

With no session cookie, `GET /account` answers `401` and the handler never
runs. With one, it answers `Hello, Ada`.

Handlers on a guarded controller take a `GuardedContext` carrying the
guard's type, where an ordinary handler takes a `Context`:

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type GuardedContext}

fn change_email(ctx: GuardedContext(User)) {
  // ctx.guard is a User here.
  controller.text(ctx, "Changing email for " <> ctx.guard.name)
}
```

Everything that reads a request, such as `query.int`, `cookie.string` and
`body.json`, accepts either kind of context.

### Finishing with build

`controller.guarded` returns a builder whose type includes the guard's
value, so a controller guarded by a `User` and one guarded by an `ApiKey`
are different types. `controller.build` finishes the builder and gives back
a plain `Controller`, so they can all be mounted on the same app:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(home_controller())
|> howdy.controller(account_controller())
```

`build` goes last, after the routes. Each route already carries its guard,
and no guard runs when the controller is built.

## Guarding one route

To protect a single route on an ordinary controller, run the guard inside
the handler with `guard.require`. It works with `use`, like the input
helpers:

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type Context}
import howdy/guard

fn show_profile(ctx: Context) {
  use user <- guard.require(ctx, authenticated)
  controller.text(ctx, user.name)
}
```

This is why `authenticated` takes a `Context(a)` rather than a plain
`Context`: written that way, one guard serves both `controller.guarded` and
`guard.require`, on any controller.

## Checks that build on a guard

`guard.require` also works inside a guarded controller, where the second
check can use the first one's value. An admin check has no need to read the
session again, because the user is already on the context:

```gleam
fn admin(ctx: GuardedContext(User)) -> service.Result(Nil) {
  case ctx.guard.admin {
    True -> Ok(Nil)
    False -> Error(service.Forbidden)
  }
}

fn delete_account(ctx: GuardedContext(User)) {
  use _ <- guard.require(ctx, admin)
  controller.status(ctx, 204)
}
```

Because `admin` asks for a `GuardedContext(User)`, it cannot be used on a
route where nobody has signed in. A signed-in visitor who is not an admin
gets `403`; one with no session never reaches the check and gets `401`.

Those two statuses are easy to mix up. `401` means "I do not know who you
are", and the fix is to sign in. `403` means "I know who you are, and you
may not do this", and signing in again will not help.

## When guards run

A guard belongs to its routes, so it only runs once a route has matched:

- A request that matches no route gets `404`, and one with the wrong method
  gets `405`, without the guard running. A guard does not hide which paths
  exist.
- Middleware runs first, outside the guard. Logging and rate limiting see a
  request even when the guard then rejects it.
- Path parameters are already captured, so a guard can read them with
  `controller.param`, to check that a visitor owns the thing they are
  asking for.
- The automatic `OPTIONS` response skips the guard. Browsers send CORS
  preflight requests without credentials, so a guard could only reject
  them.
- A WebSocket starts as a `GET` route and is guarded like one. Browsers
  cannot set headers on the handshake, so a socket guard usually reads a
  cookie or a query parameter.

## Guards or middleware?

Both run before a handler and both can reject a request. Use a guard when
the handler needs what the check found, such as the user, the API key or the
tenant, because the value arrives typed on `ctx.guard`. Use middleware when
the work is the same for every request and the handler does not care about
the result, such as logging, CORS or rate limiting.

## Testing guards

Test a guarded route from the outside, once without credentials and once
with them. `testing.cookie` adds a cookie to the request:

```gleam
import howdy/testing

pub fn account_needs_a_session_test() {
  let res = testing.get("/account") |> testing.send(app())
  assert res.status == 401
  assert testing.error(res) == Ok("unauthorized")
}

pub fn account_greets_the_user_test() {
  let res =
    testing.get("/account")
    |> testing.cookie("session", "abc")
    |> testing.send(app())
  assert testing.text(res) == "Hello, Ada"
}
```
